---
import ProjectCaseLayout from '../../components/ProjectCaseLayout.astro';
import CaseStudyTOC from '../../components/CaseStudyTOC.astro';
import { projects } from '../../data/site';

const project = projects.find(
  (project) => project.slug === 'virtual-fence-geofence'
);

if (!project) {
  throw new Error('Virtual Fence - Geofence project not found');
}

const toc = [
  { id: 'overview', label: 'Overview' },
  { id: 'contribution', label: 'Technical Contribution' },
  { id: 'challenge', label: 'The Challenge' },
  { id: 'architecture', label: 'System Architecture' },
  { id: 'sensing', label: 'Position Sensing' },
  { id: 'boundary', label: 'Boundary Calculation' },
  { id: 'integration', label: 'System Integration' },
  { id: 'skills', label: 'Engineering Skills' },
];
---

<ProjectCaseLayout
  title={project.title}
  description={project.description}
  number={project.number}
  year={project.year}
  status={project.status}
  tagline={project.tagline}
  image={project.image}
  tags={project.tags}
>

  <div slot="sidebar" class="project-links">

  <a
    class="text-link"
    href="https://github.com/EPICS-EVEI"
    target="_blank"
    rel="noreferrer"
  >
    EPICS EVEI GitHub <span>↗</span>
  </a>

  <a
    class="text-link"
    href="https://epics-evei.github.io/"
    target="_blank"
    rel="noreferrer"
  >
    Geofence Project Website <span>↗</span>
  </a>

  <CaseStudyTOC items={toc} />

  </div>


  <!-- OVERVIEW -->

  <section id="overview" class="overview">

    <p class="eyebrow">overview</p>

    <p class="big-copy">
      The Geofence is a web-based tool that allows users to define virtual boundaries
       for a remote-controlled Go-Kart using GPS coordinates. Through an interactive
        map, users can place pins to form polygons that represent either outside or inside
         boundaries, enabling the creation of safe zones with or without inner exclusions.
    </p>

    <p>
      The interface supports panning, zooming, and toggling between map views, while a
       “Show/Hide Pins” option makes pins draggable for precise adjustments. Each pin 
       is listed below the map with its latitude and longitude, and users can delete 
       individual points or clear the entire set to start fresh. Once a boundary is 
       finalized, the system generates a CSV file containing the coordinates, organized
        into outer and inner boundary sections. This CSV can be directly integrated with
         the Go-Kart’s control system, where a point-in-polygon algorithm checks the 
         kart’s real-time GPS position. If the vehicle crosses outside the designated
          safe zone, the onboard system automatically engages the brakes.
    </p>

  </section>


  <!-- MY CONTRIBUTION -->

  <section id="contribution" class="contribution">

    <p class="eyebrow">my contribution</p>

    <h2>
      Turning GPS coordinates into a boundary the go-kart could
      evaluate in real time.
    </h2>

    <p>
    My primary contribution was the geofence boundary calculation and
    its implementation within the vehicle system. I worked on the
    polygon-based logic used to determine the go-kart's position
    relative to the user-defined safe operating region.
    </p>

    <p>
    The safety logic also considered the kart's speed and projected
    motion rather than waiting until the vehicle had already crossed
    the boundary. This allowed the system to evaluate whether the kart
    was likely to leave the permitted region and initiate braking early
    enough to keep the vehicle within the designated area.
    </p>

    <p>
    I also worked with the GPS portion of the system and contributed to
    the HTML interface for the Geofence Creator. Together, these pieces
    connected user-defined geographic boundaries, real-time vehicle
    position and motion data, predictive boundary detection, and the
    automatic braking response.
    </p>

  </section>


<!-- PROBLEM -->

<section id="challenge">

  <p class="eyebrow">the challenge</p>

  <h2>
    How can a moving vehicle predict that it is about to leave an
    invisible safety boundary?
  </h2>

  <p>
    A virtual fence has no physical barrier for the go-kart to detect.
    The system therefore needed to represent the permitted operating
    region mathematically and continuously compare the kart's GPS
    position against that boundary.
  </p>

  <p>
    Detecting that the kart had already crossed the boundary would
    also be too late for a safety system. The controller needed to
    consider the vehicle's speed and motion to estimate whether it
    was approaching the boundary in a way that could carry it outside
    the permitted region.
  </p>

  <p>
    This required the full system to work together: the user defined
    the safe region, the boundary coordinates were transferred to the
    onboard system, GPS and motion data described the kart's current
    state, and the boundary logic determined whether braking needed
    to begin before the vehicle left the designated area.
  </p>

</section>


<!-- SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE -->

<section id="architecture">

  <p class="eyebrow">system architecture</p>

  <h2>
    From a browser-defined safe region to predictive automatic braking.
  </h2>

  <p>
    The system connected a web-based Geofence Creator to the embedded
    control system on the go-kart. User-defined coordinates established
    the permitted region, while onboard position and motion data were
    used to determine whether the kart could safely remain inside it.
  </p>

  <div class="flow">

    <div class="flow-box">
      <span>01</span>
      <strong>User defines the permitted operating region</strong>
    </div>

    <div class="flow-arrow">↓</div>

    <div class="flow-box">
      <span>02</span>
      <strong>Geofence Creator converts selected points into a boundary</strong>
    </div>

    <div class="flow-arrow">↓</div>

    <div class="flow-box">
      <span>03</span>
      <strong>Boundary coordinates are exported to the vehicle system</strong>
    </div>

    <div class="flow-arrow">↓</div>

    <div class="flow-box">
      <span>04</span>
      <strong>GPS and motion data describe the kart's current state</strong>
    </div>

    <div class="flow-arrow">↓</div>

    <div class="flow-box">
      <span>05</span>
      <strong>Polygon logic evaluates the kart relative to the safe region</strong>
    </div>

    <div class="flow-arrow">↓</div>

    <div class="flow-box">
      <span>06</span>
      <strong>Speed and projected motion are used to anticipate a boundary exit</strong>
    </div>

    <div class="flow-arrow">↓</div>

    <div class="flow-box">
      <span>07</span>
      <strong>Automatic braking begins before the kart leaves the safe area</strong>
    </div>

  </div>

</section>

<!-- GPS -->

<section id="sensing">
  <p class="eyebrow">position sensing</p>

  <h2>Turning vehicle position into usable safety data.</h2>

  <p>
    GPS provided the kart's real-time latitude and longitude, while the
    wider system also used IMU data to describe vehicle motion.
  </p>

  <p>
    I worked with the GPS portion of the system and the position data
    used by the boundary-detection logic.
  </p>
</section>


<!-- BOUNDARY -->

<section id="boundary">
  <p class="eyebrow">boundary calculation</p>

  <h2>Predicting whether the kart would leave the safe region.</h2>

  <p>
    My main technical contribution was the polygon-based calculation
    used to evaluate the kart's GPS position relative to the user-defined
    geofence.
  </p>

  <p>
    The system also considered speed and expected motion so braking could
    begin before the kart crossed the boundary rather than reacting after
    it had already entered an unsafe area.
  </p>

  <div class="decision">
    <div>GPS position</div>
    <span>+</span>
    <div>Polygon boundary</div>
    <span>+</span>
    <div>Speed / motion</div>
    <span>→</span>
    <div>Boundary risk</div>
  </div>

  <div class="result-grid">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">safe</p>
      <strong>Continue operation</strong>
    </div>

    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">exit predicted</p>
      <strong>Initiate braking</strong>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>


<!-- SYSTEM RESPONSE -->

<section id="integration">
  <p class="eyebrow">system integration</p>

  <h2>Connecting sensing, computation, and braking.</h2>

  <p>
    The geofence operated as part of the larger go-kart system, linking
    GPS and IMU sensing, embedded electronics, boundary calculations,
    and the automatic braking mechanism.
  </p>

  <p>
    The project showed me how a software calculation becomes useful only
    when its inputs come from real sensors and its output can drive a
    physical response.
  </p>
</section>


<!-- LEARNING -->

<section id="skills">
  <p class="eyebrow">what I learned</p>

  <ul class="learning-list">
    <li>Processing real-time GPS data for use in an embedded control system.</li>

    <li>Implementing polygon-based boundary calculations for geofencing.</li>

    <li>Using vehicle speed and projected motion to support predictive safety decisions.</li>

    <li>Designing logic around noisy, imperfect sensor measurements rather than ideal inputs.</li>

    <li>Integrating user-defined web inputs with onboard embedded calculations.</li>

    <li>Understanding interfaces between sensing, decision logic, and physical actuation.</li>

    <li>Thinking about safety requirements in terms of system response time, stopping behavior, and operating constraints.</li>
  </ul>
</section>
</ProjectCaseLayout>


<style>

  section {
    padding: 0 0 62px;
    margin: 0 0 62px;

    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  section:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
    border-bottom: 0;
  }

  section > .eyebrow {
    margin: 0 0 18px;
  }

  section p {
    max-width: 760px;

    color: #3d3e39;

    font-size: 1.08rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
  }

  .big-copy {
    margin-top: 0;

    color: var(--text);

    font-size: clamp(1.35rem, 2.4vw, 1.9rem);
    line-height: 1.4;
  }

  h2 {
    max-width: 780px;

    margin: 0 0 28px;

    font-size: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 4rem);
    line-height: 1.03;
    letter-spacing: -0.045em;

    font-weight: 580;
  }

  .content-image {
    width: 100%;

    margin: 32px 0;

    border: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .project-links {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: flex-start;

    gap: 14px;
  }

  .flow {
    max-width: 560px;

    margin-top: 32px;
  }

  .flow-box {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 50px 1fr;
    gap: 14px;

    padding: 18px 20px;

    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .flow-box span {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
  }

  .flow-arrow {
    padding: 8px 0 8px 23px;

    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .decision {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    flex-wrap: wrap;

    gap: 14px;

    margin: 34px 0;
  }

  .decision div {
    padding: 16px 18px;

    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .decision span {
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .result-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;

    gap: 14px;
  }

  .result-grid > div {
    padding: 22px;

    border: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .result-grid .eyebrow {
    margin: 0 0 10px;
  }

  .learning-list {
    padding-left: 1.2em;
  }

  .learning-list li {
    margin-bottom: 14px;

    color: #3d3e39;

    font-size: 1.08rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  @media (max-width: 760px) {

    .result-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }

  }

.contribution-grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);

  gap: 12px;

  margin-top: 34px;
}

.contribution-grid > div {
  padding: 22px;

  background: var(--surface);
  border: 1px solid var(--line);
}

.contribution-grid .num {
  display: block;

  margin-bottom: 26px;

  color: var(--muted);
  font-size: 0.72rem;
}

.contribution-grid h3 {
  margin: 0 0 12px;

  font-size: 1.15rem;
  font-weight: 580;
}

.contribution-grid p {
  margin: 0;

  font-size: 0.92rem;
  line-height: 1.55;
}

@media (max-width: 760px) {
  .contribution-grid {
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
  }
}

section {
  scroll-margin-top: 110px;

  padding: 0 0 62px;
  margin: 0 0 62px;

  border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
}

</style>